========================================================================================
 Secret count by mount and namespace, without a tree walk
========================================================================================
 The question these answer: how many secrets are in each mount, and in each namespace,
 without making tens of thousands of API calls to find out?

 STATUS: verified 2026-09-02. All 7 searches were executed against Splunk 10.4.3 and
 every one returned rows. That proves they PARSE and JOIN correctly. It does not prove
 the counts are right for YOUR estate: reconcile one mount by hand before quoting a
 number, because the inventory lookup is site-specific and a stale one reads as dormancy.

 Assumes the sample loaded as index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit with KV_MODE=json,
 and vault_secret_inventory.csv loaded as a lookup of the same name.

 Nested JSON fields are referenced as 'request.path' (single quotes) inside eval/where,
 and as request.path=... in the base search.
========================================================================================


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 WHY THIS IS A LOOKUP AND NOT AN API WALK
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Vault has no "count the secrets" endpoint. Counting means recursively listing every KV
 mount, one LIST call per directory level per mount. At a few hundred thousand secrets
 that is tens of thousands of API calls, it takes hours, and it hammers the cluster
 every time someone refreshes a dashboard. A query-time tree walk dies at that scale.

 So the walk happens ONCE, offline, and its output is a flat CSV:

     secret,namespace,mount
     "platform/secret/data/payments-0000/svc-00/credential","platform/","secret"
     "kv-apps/data/billing-0000/svc-01/credential","root","kv-apps"

 How it gets built: a one-time KV v2 metadata walk. For each namespace, `vault secrets
 list` to enumerate KV mounts, then recurse `vault kv metadata list <mount>/<dir>` to the
 leaves. Write one row per leaf. This is the same baseline inventory the stale-secret searches needs, and it is
 the ONLY tree walk in the design, which is what makes it acceptable.

 IT GOES STALE. The file is a photograph, not a mirror. Secrets created or deleted after
 the walk are wrong in it, and nothing errors: the panel just renders a number that is
 quietly out of date. Two ways to handle that, pick one and write down which:
   1. Re-run the walk on a schedule (weekly is usually enough) and reload the lookup.
   2. Keep it current between walks from audit events: KV v2 `create` and `delete`
      operations on `*/data/*` and `*/metadata/*` tell you what changed.
 Option 2 still needs option 1 periodically, because a missed event drifts forever.

 The `namespace` column writes the root namespace as the literal string "root", while
 the audit log writes it as an EMPTY STRING. Normalise before you compare the two.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5.0  Sanity check: did the lookup load, and does it look like the whole estate?
       RUN THIS FIRST. If total_secrets is far below what you expect, the walk was
       incomplete (a namespace you lacked a token for, or a mount it could not list)
       and every count below is understated with no error.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| inputlookup vault_secret_inventory.csv
| stats count AS total_secrets,
        dc(secret) AS distinct_secrets,
        dc(mount) AS mounts,
        dc(namespace) AS namespaces


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5.1  Secret count per mount, per namespace
       The headline table. No API calls, no traversal, one lookup scan.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| inputlookup vault_secret_inventory.csv
| stats dc(secret) AS secrets BY namespace mount
| sort - secrets


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5.2  Secret count per namespace, mounts rolled up
       The number a namespace owner is accountable for.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| inputlookup vault_secret_inventory.csv
| stats dc(secret) AS secrets, dc(mount) AS mounts BY namespace
| eval secrets_per_mount = round(secrets / mounts, 1)
| sort - secrets


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5.3  Secret count per mount, all namespaces
       Answers "which mount type is actually carrying the estate", which is usually a
       different answer from "which namespace has the most secrets".
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| inputlookup vault_secret_inventory.csv
| stats dc(secret) AS secrets, dc(namespace) AS namespaces BY mount
| sort - secrets


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5.4  COMPANION: distinct secrets SEEN IN AUDIT DATA, per mount
       This is the other half of the comparison and it is a completely different
       measurement. The inventory says what EXISTS. This says what something TOUCHED
       inside the search window. A secret read once a year is absent from a 30-day
       window and present in the inventory, and that is not a bug in either one.

       Mount is the first path segment of the namespace-relative request path, which is
       exactly what the inventory's `mount` column holds. Derive it, do not use
       'request.mount_point', which carries a trailing slash and will not join.

       Only .../data/... counts as use. Reads of metadata/ and subkeys/, and all list
       operations, are browsing. See rule 2 in docs/how-it-works.md.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
    request.mount_type=kv
    request.path="*/data/*"
| eval secret = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| eval namespace = if('request.namespace.path'=="" OR isnull('request.namespace.path'),
                      "root", 'request.namespace.path')
| eval mount = mvindex(split('request.path', "/"), 0)
| stats dc(secret) AS secrets_seen, count AS events, max(_time) AS last_seen
        BY namespace mount
| eval last_seen = strftime(last_seen, "%F %T")
| sort - secrets_seen


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5.5  THE PANEL THAT MATTERS: inventory vs seen, and the gap between them
       The gap is the finding. A mount holding 4,000 secrets where audit has seen 60 of
       them is not a busy mount with a reporting problem, it is 3,940 secrets nothing
       has touched in the window. Widen the window to eighteen months and the gap
       becomes the never-accessed population directly.

       Both branches are normalised to the same (namespace, mount) key before the union,
       or the two sides land on separate rows and every gap reads as 100%.

       READ THE WINDOW BEFORE YOU READ THE NUMBER. `seen` is only ever "seen within the
       search time range". A short range inflates the gap and makes the estate look far
       more dormant than it is. Set the time picker to the period you actually mean and
       say so on the panel.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
    request.mount_type=kv
    request.path="*/data/*"
| eval secret = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| eval namespace = if('request.namespace.path'=="" OR isnull('request.namespace.path'),
                      "root", 'request.namespace.path')
| eval mount = mvindex(split('request.path', "/"), 0)
| stats dc(secret) AS seen BY namespace mount

| append [
    | inputlookup vault_secret_inventory.csv
    | stats dc(secret) AS in_inventory BY namespace mount
  ]

| stats sum(seen) AS seen, sum(in_inventory) AS in_inventory BY namespace mount
| eval seen         = coalesce(seen, 0),
       in_inventory = coalesce(in_inventory, 0)
| eval never_seen   = in_inventory - seen
| eval pct_seen     = if(in_inventory > 0, round(100 * seen / in_inventory, 1), null())
| table namespace mount in_inventory seen never_seen pct_seen
| sort - never_seen

/*  Two ways this search tells you something is wrong rather than something is dormant:

    never_seen NEGATIVE  -> audit saw more distinct secrets than the inventory holds.
                            The inventory is stale (secrets created since the walk) or
                            incomplete (a mount the walk could not list). Re-walk.
    in_inventory == 0    -> a mount that only exists in audit data. Same cause, and it
                            also means the stale-secret searches's "never read" count is understating, because
                            those secrets have no baseline row to be counted against.

    Neither raises an error. Both just render.                                          */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5.6  Estate totals, one row, for a single-value panel row
       Same union as 5.5, rolled all the way up.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
    request.mount_type=kv
    request.path="*/data/*"
| eval secret = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| stats dc(secret) AS seen
| append [
    | inputlookup vault_secret_inventory.csv
    | stats dc(secret) AS in_inventory
  ]
| stats sum(seen) AS seen, sum(in_inventory) AS in_inventory
| eval never_seen_in_window = in_inventory - seen,
       pct_seen = round(100 * seen / in_inventory, 1)


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5.7  PRODUCTION NOTE: when the lookup itself gets too big
       A CSV lookup is fine into the low hundreds of thousands of rows. Past that, or if
       5.5 gets slow, convert the inventory to a KV Store collection and swap
       `| inputlookup vault_secret_inventory.csv` for `| inputlookup vault_secret_inv`
       against the collection. Nothing else in these searches changes.

       The audit side of 5.5 is a full rescan of the window on every refresh, the same
       problem 1.1 has. If the window is long, feed it from the 1.4 summary index
       instead: `index=vault_summary sourcetype=vault:secret:lastseen | stats dc(secret)
       AS seen BY namespace mount` after re-deriving mount from the stored secret key.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
